      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "The significance of ST segment shift with respect to coronary anatomy and hospital outcome was evaluated in 135 patients with unstable angina. ST shift was evident in 44% of patients on admission electrocardiogram (ECG) and in 66% on Holter monitor ECG. During hospitalization, 7% of patients had myocardial infarction, 4% died and 34% had urgent coronary revascularization. By comparing patients with and without ST shift on admission ECG, an unfavorable outcome was found in 55% versus 25% (p less than 0.005), multivessel disease in 77% versus 63% (p less than 0.05) and left main coronary artery stenosis in 22% versus 7% (p less than 0.025). When patients with and without ST shift on Holter monitor ECG were compared, an unfavorable outcome was found in 48% versus 20% (p less than 0.005), multivessel disease in 76% versus 54% (p less than 0.01) and left main coronary stenosis in 18% versus 4% (p less than 0.05). The duration of ST shift was also greater in patients with 1) unfavorable outcome (129 +/- 136 versus 52 +/- 111 min, p less than 0.01); 2) multivessel disease (98 +/- 129 versus 36 +/- 90 min, p less than 0.01); and 3) left main stenosis (150 +/- 147 versus 67 +/- 114 min, p less than 0.01).(ABSTRACT TRUNCATED AT 250 WORDS)"},
